From db383a621e13a24bd25945cf5afbc82f4da1edfd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NEMO Phase II executor <executor@nemo.local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 19:25:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] Phase II Rung 2 (scaffolding): ice-bearing network +
 bare-kernel factor

Prep for ice transport; no transport reactions yet (ice is defined and inert).

- Lift the Rung-1 coag_assert_ice_free guard: it existed only to defer ice to
  Rung 2, which is now. An ice-bearing (J-species) coagulating network is henceforth
  allowed; the transport reactions that make it correct land in the next commit.
- Factor coag_kernel_bare(i,j) (the kernel WITHOUT the 1/2 self-pair factor) out of
  coag_kernel. Grain reactions keep the 1/2 on the diagonal (coag_kernel); ice
  transport will use the bare kernel (full K_ii), because its reactants J_i X and
  GRAIN_j are distinct and each depleted once. Behaviour-preserving for grains:
  coag_kernel is unchanged, so all Rung 1/1b gates and coag-OFF byte-identity are
  unaffected (kernel check, constant + Brownian gates, jac_weight_check all green;
  13450 entries, worst reldiff 1.8006e-3).
- New tests/fixture_ice_transport/: 3-bin toy (brief's hand-checkable grid), 4 prime
  elements (H,He,C,O), inert CO ice as base species JCO (read_species expands to
  J01CO..J0NCO), all CO initially in bin 1. Confirmed empirically: the chemistry-free
  fixture inits and integrates with ice present and needs no surface scaffolding
  beyond the grid -- nb_sites_per_grain is computed from grain_radii + surface_site_
  density unconditionally, so the coverage guard is grid-sourceable (brief item 3
  resolved). Dropped-flux warnings on this tiny grid are expected and do not affect
  ice conservation (skipped-overflow pairs simply don't transport; ice stays put).
